 image(FIG/'cv_f1.png');page();heading('3.1 Development comparisons');image(FIG/'student_comparison.png');image(FIG/'logloss.png')
 p('These plots are generated directly from results/model_metrics.csv. Tuned XGBoost CV values come from the selected search trial and therefore carry optimisation bias; they are selection scores, not unbiased nested-CV estimates. No epoch curves are fabricated for algorithms without a training history.')
 descriptions={
 'logistic':('A scaled linear probability model tests whether geometric and direction evidence can be combined without complex interactions. It minimises regularised logistic loss with L2 regularisation, C=1.0, L-BFGS and at most 300 iterations.','It is fast, compact and interpretable, but a single linear decision surface cannot adapt its interactions separately at junctions, low speed and ambiguous parallel roads.'),
 'knn':('Distance-weighted KNN tests local similarity after fit-fold standardisation. It uses 25 neighbours and a deterministic uniform sample of at most 160,000 fit candidates. It has no gradient-based loss; prediction is a distance-weighted local vote smoothed as (25p+1)/27.','It can capture irregular local boundaries without fitting a parametric decision surface. Search and storage costs grow with the reference set, and fixed feature scaling can make irrelevant dimensions distort neighbourhoods.'),
 'forest':('Random Forest tests nonlinear feature interactions using 80 trees, maximum depth 16, minimum leaf size 10, square-root feature sampling and seed 261118. Trees optimise Gini impurity; averaged leaf probabilities supply candidate match scores.','Bagging stabilises nonlinear rules and captures direction-by-speed interactions. The forest uses more memory and fit time than the linear model and can overfit repeated geometric patterns despite trip-level validation.'),
 'xgboost':('XGBoost tests regularised additive tree interactions. The initial configuration uses 240 depth-5 trees, learning rate 0.07, minimum child weight 12, subsample 0.85, column fraction 0.9 and lambda 5. The objective is binary logistic loss with histogram trees trained on CUDA.','Boosting models conjunctions and produces compact probability artifacts, but can amplify reference-label noise and requires explicit regularisation and held-out-trip validation.'),
 'mlp':('The PyTorch MLP uses 13 inputs, hidden layers of 64 and 32 ReLU units and one logit. AdamW optimises binary cross entropy for 12 epochs, with batch size 16,384, learning rate 0.001 and weight decay 0.0001. Scaling is learned on the fit fold only. Actual fold training and validation loss is logged every epoch.','The network learns smooth nonlinear feature combinations and trains on CUDA. Scaling, optimisation and label noise affect probability quality; a fixed 12-epoch schedule may not be optimal for every fold.'),
 'xgboost_tuned':('The same XGBoost family is refitted with the selected Bayesian-search configuration. It is a tuned variant, not a sixth independent algorithm family. Every trial and its three fold objectives are preserved.','The search targets road-choice quality directly, but twelve trials are a small budget and using the best CV score for selection creates optimism. Student-test results are reported separately rather than described as unbiased final evaluation.')}
